1
00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:04.404
In this
lesson, you're going to try your hand

2
00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:07.407
at a few typical
applications of function calling.

3
00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:10.410
Let's start applying function calling.

4
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.747
Traditional LLMs are trained
on static datasets

5
00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:17.951
and lack the ability
to access or process information

6
00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:21.421
that has emerged
after their last training update.

7
00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:26.659
This leads to outdated or irrelevant
responses, especially in rapidly

8
00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:30.630
evolving fields like technology, medicine,
or current events.

9
00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:36.069
Function calling allows LLMs
to retrieve current data from the web

10
00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:39.339
or from your company
internal databases in real time.

11
00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:43.543
For example, a function can be call
to fetch the latest news,

12
00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:46.780
stock market updates or weather forecasts,

13
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.983
ensuring that the information provided
is up to date.

14
00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:53.920
This capability is crucial
for applications where timeliness

15
00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:57.057
and accuracy of information are essential.

16
00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:59.492
Let's dive into a few examples.

17
00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:01.694
Suppose you are interested in the events
that took place

18
00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:03.897
after you finish training your LLM.

19
00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.900
How would you adapt your LLM
into this new information?

20
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.336
For example, let's
ask about a recent product announcement.

21
00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:13.173
We will first the load_dotenv file.

22
00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:16.676
Great.

23
00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:20.880
Now you will ask about this R1
device, which was announced

24
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.918
well after the conclusion
of the training of the LLM.

25
00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:28.521
So just post the question
asking about this R1 thing

26
00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:31.524
to the LLM and submit your query.

27
00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.029
You'll notice that the LLM rejects it,

28
00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:39.032
saying it doesn't have the information
necessary.

29
00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:41.634
Let's try a web search instead.

30
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:45.605
Define a utility called do web search,
which accepts a user query

31
00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.073
and the number of results

32
00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:51.277
to circumscribe your search to
and within the tool, you will query

33
00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:55.215
the search endpoint for a web search API,

34
00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.318
which would contain a payload

35
00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:02.655
that wraps around your API key
as well as the user query.

36
00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:06.993
Submit a Post request and collate
all of the content

37
00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:11.764
from the responses into a single
string and return that string.

38
00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:14.134
Similar to earlier,

39
00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.770
you will define a function calling prompt
that Raven will use.

40
00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:23.176
Define the function annotation for the web
search utility that you defined earlier,

41
00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:28.014
which specifies the function signature
as well as a function dot string.

42
00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:32.785
You also provide a one shot example
for an example of user query and function

43
00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:37.290
call that you want Raven to use
as a reference when understanding the tool

44
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.291
that you build.

45
00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:42.462
You will then provide the user query that
you've been using since the first sale,

46
00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:46.032
and you will get back a Raven function
call.

47
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.334
Which you can execute to get

48
00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:51.571
a list of information
that your tool returns.

49
00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:56.242
You will then provide that information
returned by a tool back to the LLM,

50
00:02:56.943 --> 00:03:00.747
along with the user query
you've been using since the first cell.

51
00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.450
Provide this prompt back LLM

52
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.453
to get back a grounded response.

53
00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.159
Taking a look at the response,

54
00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:16.362
notice that it's far richer and contains
far more details,

55
00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:19.799
such as the product dimensions,

56
00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:22.936
as well as the capabilities
of the product.

57
00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:25.705
This is information that the LLM you had no idea

58
00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:29.342
about since the product was released
well past its training date.

59
00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:33.813
However, because we provided access
to the internet via the search tool,

60
00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.784
the LLM was able
to find this information and then digest

61
00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:41.054
the results and provide you
with a very concrete answer.

62
00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:44.257
Please try this yourself with your own
queries.

63
00:03:44.257 --> 00:03:48.428
Next, let's take a look at chatting
with your SQL database.

64
00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:54.901
Oftentimes, for a lot of companies,
there are insights that are lot behind

65
00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:59.339
company internal databases
and knowledge based. Because these are data

66
00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:02.642
that public portals
will not have access to,

67
00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:06.679
a lot of the public open-source language
models will not be able

68
00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.682
to give you
any meaningful answers for questions

69
00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:13.119
that depend on data
that's locked behind these sources.

70
00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:16.856
A good way of resolving
this is to provide access

71
00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:21.494
to your database, to your LLM
using function calling.

72
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:24.030
Let's take a look at making
this more concrete.

73
00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:29.030
First, create a random database
in the utils.py file found

74
00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.536
within the same folder,

75
00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:33.873
you will find a utility
called "create random database".

76
00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:38.578
This utility will create a database
or toy_database.DB,

77
00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:43.383
which will be populated with random toy
names and random toy prices.

78
00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.919
The database will create
a table called toys,

79
00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:51.658
which contains the name of the toy
as well as the price of the toy.

80
00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:56.195
You will define
another utility called "execute SQL"

81
00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:59.198
within the same utils.py file.

82
00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:03.870
This will simply take in some SQL tool
and execute it against your

83
00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:08.841
toy_database.DB, which is the database
you defined in your previous utility.

84
00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:12.879
You will import the create random
database utility and run it.

85
00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:17.650
You will then pose a question such

86
00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:21.821
as "what is the most expensive item
that your company currently sells?"

87
00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:24.657
Answering this question depends on data

88
00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:28.328
that's not behind the database
that you created earlier.

89
00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:31.397
So let's try running it
and gathering some information.

90
00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:35.034
Since the LLM doesn't
really understand the schema

91
00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:36.602
that you've defined earlier,

92
00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:40.073
let's make it concrete
and provide the schema to the LLM.

93
00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:43.443
This schema once again
just tells the LLM

94
00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:46.446
that you created a database called toys,

95
00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:50.750
which contains the name of the toy
and the price of the toy.

96
00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:54.220
You provide this schema
in your Raven prompt

97
00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:57.457
along with your function annotation.

98
00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:02.862
You also provide the user question
or the user query that you had earlier.

99
00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:07.233
Again, to the model, you will
then run the model to get the output.

100
00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:09.969
Great.

101
00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:13.906
You see that the model has returned
the SQL call that selects

102
00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:19.407
the name and the price from the database
table called toys,

103
00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:25.184
and it orders it by the price
by descending and limited to one

104
00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:30.123
which gets the most expensive item
that's currently defined in your table,

105
00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:33.393
which is the query that we want answered.

106
00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:37.096
You provide the results
you draw back earlier from your database

107
00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:40.366
back to your LLM along with the question

108
00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:43.369
that you had earlier.

109
00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.607
And you get back a response
that says that the most expensive item

110
00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:50.643
your company currently sells
is the Wonder Robot,

111
00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.913
which costs nearly $20,

112
00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:57.950
which is directly an answer
to the question that you posed earlier.

113
00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:02.922
But you'll notice that you had to allow
the LLM full access to your database.

114
00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:07.527
You allowed the LLM
to generate the raw SQL code directly.

115
00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:10.196
But what if you didn't want to do that?

116
00:07:10.196 --> 00:07:14.367
What if, due to security purposes,
you didn't want the LLM to generate

117
00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:18.137
raw SQL code
that you can execute against a database?

118
00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:22.809
There is the more gated version
that you can use for more added security.

119
00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:28.342
Instead of asking the LLM to generate
native SQL, which could be problematic,

120
00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:32.185
we can guard the access to our database
more carefully.

121
00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:35.788
Let's define a few functions
to make this a reality.

122
00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:39.425
We can allow for safer interactions
with databases.

123
00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:44.697
You will define a function to wrap around
the operations that you had in mind.

124
00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:46.199
First,

125
00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:49.802
let's define a function
to connect to your database.

126
00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:53.439
You define a function to list all toys
in your database

127
00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:56.709
and implement the SQL behind the scenes.

128
00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:01.247
Similarly,
to find toys by prefix and define toys

129
00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:04.283
in a price range. To get random toys.

130
00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:08.054
To get the most expensive toy.

131
00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.523
To get the cheapest toy.

132
00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:13.459
And finally,
you will provide all of the functions

133
00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:17.163
you defined earlier to Raven
using the function

134
00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:20.166
annotation format.

135
00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:22.168
Since Raven doesn't

136
00:08:22.168 --> 00:08:26.038
have direct access to your database,
you need not provide

137
00:08:26.038 --> 00:08:29.675
Raven with the database schema
that you've designed earlier.

138
00:08:30.076 --> 00:08:34.113
Rather, Raven would just use the templates
that you've provided to it

139
00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:37.550
to answer the user query.

140
00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:41.387
Let's run this and see the output.

141
00:08:42.655 --> 00:08:43.322
Great!

142
00:08:43.322 --> 00:08:45.925
Raven was able to interface
with your database

143
00:08:45.925 --> 00:08:49.962
and pull out the information
necessary to answer the user query.

144
00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.232
And you can provide the results back

145
00:08:53.232 --> 00:08:57.003
to Raven to get your original answer
to your original query.

146
00:08:57.503 --> 00:09:00.907
Which is a wonder robot
which costs nearly $20.

147
00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:03.476
Great.

148
00:09:03.476 --> 00:09:08.476
Try to create a query to use one of the other
functions, we've defined several.

149
00:09:09.315 --> 00:09:13.419
Please try queries that can leverage
some of these other functions

150
00:09:13.419 --> 00:09:15.421
that we've defined earlier.

151
00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:18.891
Now, you've seen how you can use
Raven and other function calling

152
00:09:18.891 --> 00:09:23.891
LLMs and grounded with access
to the internet via web search

153
00:09:24.564 --> 00:09:27.567
or to your database via native SQL,

154
00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:30.670
or via the safer templated approach

155
00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:35.341
to get concrete answers
to your user queries, which might depend

156
00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:39.278
on company internal data
or the most up to date happenings.

157
00:09:39.812 --> 00:09:42.715
In the next lesson,
we will start taking a look

158
00:09:42.715 --> 00:09:46.319
at further pushing
the capabilities of function calling LLMs.

159
00:09:46.686 --> 00:09:49.055
By taking a look at structured
extraction.

160
00:09:49.055 --> 00:09:52.091
We've learned a lot through
the entire progression of this course.

161
00:09:52.391 --> 00:09:53.526
In our next lesson,

162
